/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `conversation_messages` DISABLE KEYS */;
INSERT INTO `conversation_messages` (`id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, `content`, `model`, `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `credits_charged`, `attachments`, `mode_switch`, `created_at`) VALUES (1,1,'user','I have 30 published posts and no written voice guide. How do I work out what our voice actually is, rather than what I would like it to be?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-06-26 10:00:00'),(2,1,'assistant','Read the five posts that performed best and the five that fell flat, and note only the mechanical differences: sentence length, whether you open with a claim or a scene, how often you use \"we\", whether examples are named or generic.\n\nWhat you will almost certainly find is that your voice is already consistent and just undocumented. Write the guide from the evidence — \"we open with the claim\", \"we name the client or use no example at all\" — not from adjectives. A guide that says \"friendly but authoritative\" cannot be followed by anyone, including you.','claude-sonnet-4-6',125,139,1.0600,NULL,NULL,'2026-06-26 10:01:33'),(3,1,'user','Turn that into six rules I can put in front of a freelancer.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-06-26 10:06:00'),(4,1,'assistant','1. Open with the claim. No scene-setting paragraph.\n2. One idea per paragraph, three sentences maximum.\n3. Name the company in every example, or use no example.\n4. Never \"unlock\", \"leverage\", \"game-changing\", \"in today\'s fast-paced\".\n5. Numbers beat adjectives: \"cut drafting to 40 minutes\", not \"dramatically faster\".\n6. End on the next action, not a summary of what was just read.\n\nSix is about the limit of what a freelancer will actually hold in their head. Anything else belongs in edit notes.','claude-sonnet-4-6',365,125,1.9600,NULL,NULL,'2026-06-26 10:06:31'),(5,2,'user','Our pricing page has three tiers and a feature grid with 22 rows. People are bouncing. What do I cut?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 14:00:00'),(6,2,'assistant','Cut the grid to the six rows that differ between tiers. The other sixteen are in every plan, so all they do is make the reader work to find the ones that matter.\n\nThen put one sentence above each tier naming who it is for — \"for a solo founder publishing weekly\", \"for a studio with clients\". Most people cannot map a feature list onto themselves, but they recognise a description of their own situation immediately.','gpt-5.6-terra',116,104,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 14:00:34'),(7,2,'user','Should the annual discount be shown as a percentage or as two months free?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 14:05:00'),(8,2,'assistant','Two months free. Same arithmetic, but it is a concrete thing rather than a rate the reader has to apply to a number they have not committed to yet.\n\nShow the monthly-equivalent price underneath in smaller text so anyone comparing against a competitor\'s monthly figure can do it without a calculator.','gpt-5.6-terra',369,75,1.7800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 14:06:27'),(9,3,'user','New client, mid-market logistics SaaS. Draft the structure of an onboarding brief I can send before our kickoff call.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 11:00:00'),(10,3,'assistant','Keep it to one page and make every section a question they answer, not a box you fill:\n\n1. **Who is the buyer, and who else has to agree?** — the second half is where B2B content usually goes wrong.\n2. **What are they doing today instead of buying you?** — the real competitor is almost never the named one.\n3. **Which three claims can you prove?** — with the proof attached.\n4. **What must we never say?** — legal, positioning, past mistakes.\n5. **Who signs off, and by when?** — one name.\n\nSend it 48 hours before the call so the answers arrive cold rather than being improvised live.','claude-sonnet-4-6',120,147,1.0700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 11:01:04'),(11,3,'user','They came back with nothing for question 3. How hard do I push?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 11:05:00'),(12,3,'assistant','Push once, in the call, by asking it differently: \"what is the last thing a customer told you that surprised you?\" People who cannot list provable claims can almost always retell a customer conversation.\n\nIf that also comes up empty, treat it as the finding rather than the blocker — it means the first piece of work is a customer interview, not a blog post, and you should quote it that way.','claude-sonnet-4-6',366,98,1.8600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 11:06:07'),(13,4,'user','Plan a content cluster around AI workflow templates. We have no domain authority to speak of.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 15:00:00'),(14,4,'assistant','With no authority, skip the head term entirely for now.\n\nOne pillar page on \"AI workflow templates\" for later, then five comparison and five \"how do I actually\" pages targeting phrasings with obvious intent and thin competition — \"AI workflow for client onboarding\", \"content approval workflow template\". Those rank on relevance rather than links.\n\nInternally link every spoke to the pillar and to two siblings. The cluster is what eventually carries the head term; publishing the pillar first just gets it ignored.','sonar-pro',114,129,0.9700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 15:01:31'),(15,4,'user','How long before I should expect to see anything?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 15:03:00'),(16,4,'assistant','Ten to sixteen weeks for the long-tail spokes to settle, longer for the pillar. Anything that moves inside three weeks is usually a temporary freshness boost, not a position — do not rewrite your plan on the strength of it.\n\nThe leading indicator worth watching earlier is impressions on the spoke queries. Impressions rising with poor position means the topic is right and the page is not.','sonar-pro',362,98,1.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 15:03:35'),(17,5,'user','We moved the newsletter from weekly to fortnightly. Opens went up 4 points, total clicks went down. Which number should I care about?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 16:00:00'),(18,5,'assistant','Total clicks, but not for the obvious reason.\n\nOpen rate rose because you sent to a list that had longer to forget it was tired of you — that is a denominator effect, not renewed interest. Total clicks fell because you halved the number of chances to be clicked.\n\nThe number that settles it is clicks per subscriber per month, and unsubscribes per send. If fortnightly holds clicks per subscriber steady while dropping unsubscribes, it is a genuine win. If clicks per subscriber fell too, you just published less.','gemini-3.5-flash',124,129,0.7600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 16:00:34'),(19,6,'user','Here are last quarter\'s email results. Where should the launch sequence differ from what we have been sending?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,'[{\"id\": \"01KZ972MTV2G4KYVCJXHQ5V6Q7\", \"name\": \"email-performance-q2.csv\", \"size\": 187, \"type\": \"text/csv\", \"extension\": \"csv\", \"storage_path\": \"chat-attachments/2/01KZ972MTV2G4KYVCJXHQ5V6Q7.csv\", \"text_content\": \"week,sent,open_rate,click_rate,replies\\n2026-05-04,1840,41.2%,6.8%,12\\n2026-05-11,1902,38.7%,5.1%,9\\n2026-05-18,1955,44.9%,9.3%,21\\n2026-05-25,1988,29.4%,3.2%,4\\n2026-06-01,2031,43.1%,8.7%,18\\n\"}]',NULL,'2026-07-30 09:00:00'),(20,6,'assistant','Two things stand out in that file.\n\nThe 18 May send doubled every other week\'s replies at a similar volume, and the 25 May send collapsed on all three metrics — worth checking what those two had in common before writing anything new.\n\nFor the launch itself: your click rate lives between 3% and 9% on a stable list, so a four-email sequence is realistic and a seven-email one will burn goodwill you need for the follow-up. Announcement, one objection, one proof, one last call.','gpt-5.6-terra',118,120,0.9500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:01:33'),(21,6,'user','Write the objection email. The objection is \"we already have a process for this\".',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:04:00'),(22,6,'assistant','> Subject: you already have a process\n>\n> You do. It works, it is in someone\'s head, and it survives contact with a busy week — mostly.\n>\n> The teams that switched were not replacing a broken process. They were replacing the part of a working one that only exists because one person remembers it: which draft is current, who still has to approve, what the client said in March.\n>\n> If that person is you, this is worth fifteen minutes. If it is someone else, forward it to them.\n\nGranting the objection in the first line is the whole trick. Arguing with it makes the reader defend a process they were mildly unhappy with.','gpt-5.6-terra',371,156,2.1100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:04:35'),(23,6,'user','Give me a subject line alternative that is less clever.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:12:00'),(24,6,'assistant','\"What happens when the person who remembers is on holiday.\"\n\nPlainer, same argument, and it survives being read in a notification preview — which is where most of these are actually judged.','gpt-5.6-terra',624,48,2.6900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:13:06'),(25,7,'user','Two competitors moved features from their top tier down to entry level this quarter. What does that usually mean?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 13:00:00'),(26,7,'assistant','Usually one of two things, and they look identical from outside.\n\nEither the feature stopped differentiating — someone cheaper shipped it, so holding it back only cost them entry-level conversions. Or their top tier needed room for something new and this was pushed down to make space.\n\nCheck whether the top tier gained anything in the same release. If it did, it is repositioning and you have a quarter or two. If it did not, the feature is now table stakes and you are already late.','gpt-5.6-terra',119,122,0.9600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 13:00:38'),(27,8,'user','Turn these call notes into owned tasks with dates: they want the quarterly report restructured, the case study is blocked on legal, and they asked about adding a second language.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 17:00:00'),(28,8,'assistant','**Us, this week** — restructure the quarterly report. Send the new outline for approval before writing, so the rewrite is not done twice.\n\n**Them, this week** — one named person to chase legal on the case study. A task assigned to \"legal\" is a task assigned to nobody; ask for the name in writing today.\n\n**Nobody, deliberately** — the second language. It is a scoping question, not a task, and turning it into one now commits you to an estimate you cannot make. Put it on the next agenda instead.\n\nThe third item is the one that matters. Vague asks become deadlines by accident when everything on a list looks the same.','claude-sonnet-4-6',135,155,1.1600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 17:01:06'),(29,8,'user','They will push on the language question. Give me a one-line holding answer.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 17:03:00'),(30,8,'assistant','\"Happy to scope it — the answer depends on whether you need the existing 40 pages translated or only new work, and those are very different projects. Can we take fifteen minutes on it next week?\"\n\nIt is a real question rather than a stall, which is why it holds.','claude-sonnet-4-6',369,66,1.7400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 17:04:24'),(31,9,'user','Cut the launch blog post into a LinkedIn post and three shorter follow-ups.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 11:00:00'),(32,9,'assistant','Main post: the number that made you build it, then what you did about it, then one line on what is still unsolved. Admitting the unsolved part is what stops it reading as an advert.\n\nFollow-ups, spread over two weeks:\n1. The objection you heard most in beta, answered honestly.\n2. One screenshot with the reasoning behind a single design decision.\n3. A customer\'s own sentence, quoted, with no commentary from you.\n\nDo not post them on consecutive days — the third lands on people who have not seen the first.','gpt-5.4-mini',109,128,0.4700,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 11:00:42'),(33,10,'user','Five retargeting hooks for people who read the pricing page and left.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:00:00'),(34,10,'assistant','1. \"You looked at the pricing. Here is what the middle tier actually includes.\"\n2. \"Cheaper than the freelancer you were about to email.\"\n3. \"Two months free if you pay yearly. That is the whole offer.\"\n4. \"Still comparing? Here is the honest version of us versus them.\"\n5. \"Forty minutes per article, or your month back.\"\n\nNumber 4 will outperform the rest and make you uncomfortable, which is usually the same thing. Number 2 only works if it is true — check your actual rates before running it.','claude-haiku-4-5',108,125,0.4700,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:01:23'),(35,11,'user','Our hero reads \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". Give me something a visitor can test in three seconds.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:20:00'),(36,11,'assistant','\"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\"\n\nThe current line describes a category. This one makes a claim the reader can check against their own week, which is the only thing a hero has time to do.\n\nPut the proof directly beneath it rather than three sections down: one customer sentence, one number, nothing else.','gpt-5.6-terra',118,91,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:20:37'),(37,11,'user','The founder thinks it undersells the AI part. Push back or fold?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:27:00'),(38,11,'assistant','Push back, but not on taste — on evidence.\n\nEveryone in the category leads with AI, so leading with it makes you indistinguishable from the eleven other tabs the visitor has open. The outcome is what differs. Offer to run both as an A/B test on the hero alone and let the number decide; that usually ends the argument faster than the argument does.\n\nIf they still want AI in the hero, put it in the subhead where it costs nothing.','gpt-5.6-terra',366,108,1.9000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:27:50'),(39,12,'user','Our hero reads \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". Give me something a visitor can test in three seconds.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 10:05:00'),(40,12,'assistant','\"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\"\n\nThe current line describes a category. This one makes a claim the reader can check against their own week, which is the only thing a hero has time to do.\n\nPut the proof directly beneath it rather than three sections down: one customer sentence, one number, nothing else.','claude-sonnet-4-6',118,91,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 10:05:25'),(41,12,'user','Different direction from here: keep the category framing but make it specific. What would that look like?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 10:11:00'),(42,12,'assistant','\"The content workflow for studios that publish every week.\"\n\nStill a category line, but a category of one — it names the cadence and the buyer instead of the technology. A founder who wants the positioning-led hero can usually live with this, because it sounds like a claim about the market rather than a claim about a feature.\n\nIt will convert slightly worse than the outcome line and considerably better than what is up there now.','claude-sonnet-4-6',377,108,1.9400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 10:11:55'),(43,13,'user','Second onboarding email. They signed up, they have not generated anything yet. What is the job of this email?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 12:40:00'),(44,13,'assistant','One job: get them to produce a single output, however small.\n\nSo do not summarise the product. Pick the shortest useful thing they can finish in two minutes — a meta description, a subject line — and make the whole email about that one action, with the link going straight into it rather than to the dashboard.\n\nThe account that generates once in week one is a different retention curve from the account that reads three emails and never does. Everything else you might say fits better in email four.','claude-sonnet-4-6',118,125,0.9700,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 12:40:55'),(45,14,'user','My queued jobs are marked failed but nothing lands in the failed_jobs table. Laravel 12, Redis driver.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:07:00'),(46,14,'assistant','Almost always one of three things:\n\n1. The worker was started before the failed-job table existed — `php artisan queue:failed-table` then migrate, and restart the worker.\n2. `queue.failed.driver` is set to `null` in config (common in a copied .env).\n3. The job throws in `__construct`, which happens on dispatch, not in the worker — that exception goes to your normal log, not the failed table.\n\nCheck `config(\'queue.failed\')` first; it is the cheapest of the three to rule out.','gemini-3.5-flash',106,120,0.6800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:08:11'),(47,14,'user','It was the null driver. How do I get an alert when a job fails now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:10:00'),(48,14,'assistant','Hook the `JobFailed` event in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`:\n\n```php\nQueue::failing(function (JobFailed $event) {\n    Log::critical(\'Job failed\', [\n        \'connection\' => $event->connectionName,\n        \'job\' => $event->job->resolveName(),\n        \'exception\' => $event->exception->getMessage(),\n    ]);\n});\n```\n\nPoint that log channel at Slack or your error tracker rather than building a notification path of your own.','gemini-3.5-flash',317,105,1.2700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:11:05'),(49,14,'user','Should I set tries or backoff on the job class?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:15:00'),(50,14,'assistant','Set both, and set them per job rather than globally. `public $tries = 3;` with `public array $backoff = [10, 60, 300];` gives you three attempts spread over roughly six minutes, which covers a provider blipping without hammering it. For anything that charges money on each attempt, use `$tries = 1` and requeue deliberately.','gemini-3.5-flash',532,81,1.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 10:16:25'),(51,15,'user','I have a 600-line controller handling checkout. Where do I start splitting it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 15:29:00'),(52,15,'assistant','Split by reason to change, not by line count.\n\nStart with the three things a checkout controller usually conflates: request validation (move to a FormRequest), the payment call and its failure handling (a service), and the post-payment side effects — invoice, email, credits (listeners on one event). What is left in the controller should read as five or six lines you can take in at a glance.\n\nDo the FormRequest first: it is the lowest-risk move and it usually deletes 100 lines on its own.','gemini-3.5-flash',100,123,0.6700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 15:30:24'),(53,15,'user','How do I keep the refactor safe without a full test suite?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 15:32:00'),(54,15,'assistant','Write one characterisation test per branch you are about to move — it does not have to be pretty, only faithful to current behaviour. Then move code in commits small enough that reverting one is painless. Resist renaming anything in the same commit as a move; a diff that both moves and renames is unreviewable.','gemini-3.5-flash',315,78,1.1800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07 15:33:19'),(55,16,'user','What does /^(?!.*\\.\\.)[\\w.-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i actually match?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 09:27:00'),(56,16,'assistant','Piece by piece:\n\n- `(?!.*\\.\\.)` — a negative lookahead rejecting anything containing two consecutive dots.\n- `[\\w.-]+` — the local part: letters, digits, underscore, dot, hyphen.\n- `@[\\w-]+` — a domain label without dots.\n- `\\.[a-z]{2,}` — a dot and a TLD of two or more letters.\n- `/i` — case-insensitive.\n\nSo it matches `first.last@example.com` but not `a..b@example.com` or `user@sub.example.com` — note the single-label domain, which is probably a bug for you.','gpt-5.6-terra',97,116,0.8500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 09:28:06'),(57,17,'user','Rewrite this so it is shorter and less breathless: \"We are incredibly excited to finally announce the launch of our brand new AI-powered platform which we have been working on for many months.\"',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 09:54:00'),(58,17,'assistant','\"Our AI platform is live today.\"\n\nThat is the whole sentence. Excitement belongs in what the product does next, not in an adverb before it — readers discount \"incredibly excited\" automatically, and \"finally\" tells them you were late.\n\nIf you want one line of context: \"Our AI platform is live today, after eight months in private beta with 40 teams.\" The number does the work the adjectives were trying to do.','gemini-3.5-flash',129,103,0.7000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 09:54:35'),(59,17,'user','Now give me the opening paragraph that follows it.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 09:59:00'),(60,17,'assistant','\"It does one thing: turns the research you already have into publishable drafts, in your voice, in about a minute. No prompt engineering, no blank page. Paste your notes, pick a format, edit what comes back.\"\n\nThree sentences, one claim each — capability, cost, action. Everything else you want to say belongs further down, where the readers who are still with you will find it.','gemini-3.5-flash',313,95,1.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 10:00:04'),(61,18,'user','Write a cold email to agency owners about our AI content tool. Every version I write sounds like spam.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 10:28:00'),(62,18,'assistant','It sounds like spam because it opens with you. Try:\n\n> Subject: your October case study\n>\n> Saw the retail piece you published last week — the before/after structure is the clearest I have read this year.\n>\n> We built something that produces that structure\'s first draft from a client interview transcript. Agencies using it cut drafting from a day to about forty minutes; the editing judgement stays yours.\n>\n> Worth fifteen minutes?\n\nOne specific observation, one concrete number, one small ask. The observation has to be real — that is the part that cannot be templated.','gemini-3.5-flash',106,144,0.7500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 10:29:11'),(63,18,'user','What is a reasonable follow-up if they do not reply?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 10:33:00'),(64,18,'assistant','One follow-up, five business days later, shorter than the first and adding something rather than asking again:\n\n> Sent this over last week. Since then we published the transcript-to-draft walkthrough — here it is, in case it is more useful than a call.\n\nThen stop. A third email converts almost nobody and costs you the address.','gemini-3.5-flash',313,82,1.1900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-08 10:33:30'),(65,19,'user','I have a 40-page quarterly report. What is the best way to get a usable summary out of it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 16:22:00'),(66,19,'assistant','Do not ask for \"a summary\" — ask for the shape you need. Three that work:\n\n1. **Decision brief**: what changed, what it means, what someone must now decide. Half a page.\n2. **Number sheet**: every figure that moved more than 10%, with its prior value.\n3. **Exception list**: only what contradicts last quarter\'s expectations.\n\nGenerate all three from the same document and read the exception list first — that is where anything worth acting on hides.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',103,113,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 16:23:10'),(67,20,'user','A competitor launched at half our price and we are losing deals. How should we respond?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 08:07:00'),(68,20,'assistant','Do not match the price; you cannot win a race you started second in.\n\nFind the customers for whom the cheaper product is expensive — the ones whose cost of a bad output is high (regulated copy, client deliverables, anything with a legal review downstream). Reposition around that risk, not around features.\n\nConcretely: change the comparison page from a feature grid to a \"what happens when it is wrong\" section. Buyers who ignore that section were never going to pay your price anyway.','gemini-3.5-flash',102,122,0.6700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 08:07:52'),(69,21,'user','Our landing page converts at 0.8%. The hero says \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". What is wrong?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 12:46:00'),(70,21,'assistant','The hero makes no claim a visitor can test. \"The future of content\" describes a category, not what happens if they sign up.\n\nReplace it with the outcome and the constraint: \"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\" Then put the proof directly under it, not three sections down.\n\n0.8% on a page with a category-level headline is normal. Fixing the headline usually moves it more than any redesign will.','claude-haiku-4-5',106,113,0.4400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-09 12:46:23'),(71,22,'user','Turn our post about AI writing workflows into a LinkedIn post that does not read like an ad.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:53:00'),(72,22,'assistant','> We measured where the hour actually goes when our team writes an article.\n>\n> Drafting: 12 minutes. Research: 20. Deciding what the piece is about: 28.\n>\n> We had automated the 12.\n>\n> Everything we have shipped since then targets the 28 — outline pressure-testing, angle comparison, source triage. It is less impressive in a demo and considerably more useful.\n\nOne surprising number, one admission, one implication. No CTA — comments do that work for you on LinkedIn.','gemini-3.5-flash',103,118,0.6600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:53:20'),(73,22,'user','Give me three hooks I could A/B against that opener.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:55:00'),(74,22,'assistant','1. \"Automating the writing was the easy part. It was also the wrong part.\"\n2. \"We timed every step of writing one article. The result changed our roadmap.\"\n3. \"Twelve minutes of drafting. Twenty-eight minutes of deciding. Guess which one we automated first.\"\n\nThe third tends to win because it makes the reader answer before they scroll.','gemini-3.5-flash',313,85,1.1900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:55:38'),(75,23,'user','Plan two weeks of social posts leading into a launch. B2B SaaS, small audience.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:32:00'),(76,23,'assistant','Week one, build the problem: three posts on the pain, each with a number or a story, none mentioning the product. One customer quote. One \"here is how we work today\" behind-the-scenes.\n\nWeek two, close the loop: teaser with a real screenshot (day 8), the reasoning behind one design decision (day 10), launch (day 12), then two days of answering replies in public rather than posting anything new.\n\nWith a small audience the replies matter more than the posts. Budget time for them.','claude-sonnet-4-6',100,121,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-10 08:33:12'),(77,24,'user','Monthly churn jumped from 3.1% to 5.4%. Where do I look first?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 13:41:00'),(78,24,'assistant','Segment before you theorise. Split the churned accounts by:\n\n- **Tenure** — a spike concentrated in month-two accounts is onboarding; spread evenly across tenures is pricing or a competitor.\n- **Plan** — if it is one tier, look at what changed in that tier\'s limits.\n- **Acquisition channel** — a bad cohort from one campaign can move the whole number.\n\nOne of those three almost always isolates it. If none do, check for an involuntary-churn spike: expired cards and failed renewals show up as churn but are a billing problem.','gemini-3.5-flash',96,132,0.6800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 13:41:29'),(79,24,'user','It is concentrated in month two on the mid tier. What now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 13:43:00'),(80,24,'assistant','That is an expectation gap, not a product gap: they bought something in month one and did not find it by month two.\n\nPull the last 20 churned mid-tier accounts and check which features they never touched. If a feature that justifies the tier\'s price went unused in 15 of 20, the onboarding never routed them to it — that is a sequencing fix, not a discount.','gemini-3.5-flash',315,90,1.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 13:43:28'),(81,25,'user','Credits versus flat unlimited for an AI product — which should we pick?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 14:06:00'),(82,25,'assistant','They fail differently, and you should pick the failure you can survive.\n\n**Credits** align revenue with cost, so a heavy user cannot bankrupt you — but they add friction to every action and buyers hate metering they cannot predict.\n\n**Flat unlimited** sells better and forecasts worse; your margin is hostage to your top 5% of users, and one automation-happy customer can eat a tier\'s profit.\n\nMost products land on flat pricing with a fair-use ceiling nobody normal reaches. That gets the sales advantage while keeping the escape hatch.','claude-sonnet-4-6',98,135,0.9300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-11 14:06:33'),(83,26,'user','Our dashboard looks dead for new users. What should empty states actually do?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 10:20:00'),(84,26,'assistant','An empty state has one job: make the next action obvious and small.\n\nDrop the illustration-plus-\"Nothing here yet\" pattern. Instead show the panel as it will look with data, greyed at 40% opacity, with one button that produces exactly that. People understand a preview instantly and a description never.\n\nIf the action needs setup, show the count of steps left, not a wall of prose.','gpt-5.4-mini',100,96,0.3900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 10:21:24'),(85,26,'user','How many actions should the empty dashboard offer?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 10:24:00'),(86,26,'assistant','One primary, one escape hatch. \"Generate your first article\" plus a quiet \"Import existing content\" link. Three equal buttons is a menu, and a menu on an empty screen is a decision the user is not equipped to make yet.','gpt-5.4-mini',313,55,0.7400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 10:25:05'),(87,27,'user','How do I check whether our brand blue works for body text?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 17:23:00'),(88,27,'assistant','Contrast ratio against the background it sits on: 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for text above ~24px or bold above ~19px, and 3:1 for the non-text parts of a control such as an input border.\n\nBrand blues usually fail on white for body copy and pass for headings. The fix is not abandoning the colour — it is keeping the brand blue for large text and interactive fills, and using a darkened variant for anything paragraph-sized.','gpt-5.6-terra',95,106,0.8000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 17:24:02'),(89,28,'user','What should I actually record when scanning competitor features?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 16:50:00'),(90,28,'assistant','Record four columns and ignore everything else: the feature, the tier it is gated behind, when it shipped, and whether their marketing leads with it.\n\nThe gating and the date are the informative pair. A feature that shipped 18 months ago and is still enterprise-only is a moat; one that moved down to the entry tier last quarter is table stakes you now have to match.','claude-sonnet-4-6',96,92,0.7500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 16:51:24'),(91,28,'user','How often is it worth redoing this?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 16:55:00'),(92,28,'assistant','Quarterly for the full scan, with changelog subscriptions in between. Monthly scans mostly capture noise, and anything faster than that becomes a report nobody reads.','claude-sonnet-4-6',309,42,1.4000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-12 16:56:07'),(93,29,'user','I have five features requested by customers and time for one. How do I choose?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 11:50:00'),(94,29,'assistant','Ask what each one is evidence of rather than how many people asked.\n\nFor each request, write down: which customer segment asked, what they were trying to do when they hit the wall, and what they are doing instead today. Requests with a workaround already in place are lower urgency than they sound — the pain is real but survivable.\n\nThen pick the one where the workaround is worst, not the one with the most votes. Vote counts measure how many people bothered to ask, which correlates with how loud a segment is, not how much it matters.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,135,0.9400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 11:50:55'),(95,29,'user','Two of them tie on that test. Tiebreaker?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 11:54:00'),(96,29,'assistant','Build the one that teaches you more. At a tie on customer value, the option that resolves a question about your product\'s direction is worth more than the one that confirms what you already believe — you will make the next five decisions with what it tells you.','gpt-5.6-terra',311,66,1.5100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 11:55:24'),(97,30,'user','Write me an image prompt for a hero banner for an AI writing tool.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 09:22:00'),(98,30,'assistant','> Wide editorial hero image: a clean desk at dawn, a single open laptop with a soft-glowing screen, warm side light from a window on the left, muted navy and amber palette, shallow depth of field, generous empty space on the right for headline text, photographic, no text, no logos, no hands.\n\nThe two clauses doing the real work are the empty space on the right and \"no text\" — models will happily fill your headline area with garbled lettering otherwise.','deepseek-v4-pro',97,114,0.2100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-13 09:23:09'),(99,31,'user','There are a dozen models in the picker. How do I choose one?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-14 16:53:00'),(100,31,'assistant','Pick by task shape, not by benchmark:\n\n- **Long reasoning, code, anything you will ship** — the largest model you can afford; the credit difference is smaller than the time you lose re-checking a cheap answer.\n- **Bulk work — captions, tags, rewrites** — a fast, cheap model. Quality differences vanish at short output lengths.\n- **Anything needing current information** — a search-backed model, or the answer will be confidently out of date.\n\nWhen unsure, run the same prompt through a cheap and an expensive model once. The gap on your work is usually different from the gap on the leaderboard.','claude-sonnet-4-6',95,149,0.9800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-14 16:53:49'),(101,32,'user','My queued jobs are marked failed but nothing lands in the failed_jobs table. Laravel 12, Redis driver.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 09:54:00'),(102,32,'assistant','Almost always one of three things:\n\n1. The worker was started before the failed-job table existed — `php artisan queue:failed-table` then migrate, and restart the worker.\n2. `queue.failed.driver` is set to `null` in config (common in a copied .env).\n3. The job throws in `__construct`, which happens on dispatch, not in the worker — that exception goes to your normal log, not the failed table.\n\nCheck `config(\'queue.failed\')` first; it is the cheapest of the three to rule out.','gpt-5.4-mini',106,120,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 09:55:01'),(103,32,'user','It was the null driver. How do I get an alert when a job fails now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 09:59:00'),(104,32,'assistant','Hook the `JobFailed` event in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`:\n\n```php\nQueue::failing(function (JobFailed $event) {\n    Log::critical(\'Job failed\', [\n        \'connection\' => $event->connectionName,\n        \'job\' => $event->job->resolveName(),\n        \'exception\' => $event->exception->getMessage(),\n    ]);\n});\n```\n\nPoint that log channel at Slack or your error tracker rather than building a notification path of your own.','gpt-5.4-mini',317,105,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 10:00:23'),(105,32,'user','Should I set tries or backoff on the job class?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 10:04:00'),(106,32,'assistant','Set both, and set them per job rather than globally. `public $tries = 3;` with `public array $backoff = [10, 60, 300];` gives you three attempts spread over roughly six minutes, which covers a provider blipping without hammering it. For anything that charges money on each attempt, use `$tries = 1` and requeue deliberately.','gpt-5.4-mini',532,81,1.2300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 10:05:26'),(107,33,'user','I have a 600-line controller handling checkout. Where do I start splitting it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 08:09:00'),(108,33,'assistant','Split by reason to change, not by line count.\n\nStart with the three things a checkout controller usually conflates: request validation (move to a FormRequest), the payment call and its failure handling (a service), and the post-payment side effects — invoice, email, credits (listeners on one event). What is left in the controller should read as five or six lines you can take in at a glance.\n\nDo the FormRequest first: it is the lowest-risk move and it usually deletes 100 lines on its own.','gemini-3.5-flash',100,123,0.6700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 08:09:59'),(109,33,'user','How do I keep the refactor safe without a full test suite?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 08:13:00'),(110,33,'assistant','Write one characterisation test per branch you are about to move — it does not have to be pretty, only faithful to current behaviour. Then move code in commits small enough that reverting one is painless. Resist renaming anything in the same commit as a move; a diff that both moves and renames is unreviewable.','gemini-3.5-flash',315,78,1.1800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-15 08:14:08'),(111,34,'user','What does /^(?!.*\\.\\.)[\\w.-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i actually match?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 13:43:00'),(112,34,'assistant','Piece by piece:\n\n- `(?!.*\\.\\.)` — a negative lookahead rejecting anything containing two consecutive dots.\n- `[\\w.-]+` — the local part: letters, digits, underscore, dot, hyphen.\n- `@[\\w-]+` — a domain label without dots.\n- `\\.[a-z]{2,}` — a dot and a TLD of two or more letters.\n- `/i` — case-insensitive.\n\nSo it matches `first.last@example.com` but not `a..b@example.com` or `user@sub.example.com` — note the single-label domain, which is probably a bug for you.','grok-4.5',97,116,0.4300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 13:44:10'),(113,35,'user','Rewrite this so it is shorter and less breathless: \"We are incredibly excited to finally announce the launch of our brand new AI-powered platform which we have been working on for many months.\"',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 07:13:00'),(114,35,'assistant','\"Our AI platform is live today.\"\n\nThat is the whole sentence. Excitement belongs in what the product does next, not in an adverb before it — readers discount \"incredibly excited\" automatically, and \"finally\" tells them you were late.\n\nIf you want one line of context: \"Our AI platform is live today, after eight months in private beta with 40 teams.\" The number does the work the adjectives were trying to do.','claude-haiku-4-5',129,103,0.4600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 07:13:50'),(115,35,'user','Now give me the opening paragraph that follows it.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 07:16:00'),(116,35,'assistant','\"It does one thing: turns the research you already have into publishable drafts, in your voice, in about a minute. No prompt engineering, no blank page. Paste your notes, pick a format, edit what comes back.\"\n\nThree sentences, one claim each — capability, cost, action. Everything else you want to say belongs further down, where the readers who are still with you will find it.','claude-haiku-4-5',313,95,0.8200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-16 07:16:50'),(117,36,'user','Write a cold email to agency owners about our AI content tool. Every version I write sounds like spam.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-17 09:16:00'),(118,36,'assistant','It sounds like spam because it opens with you. Try:\n\n> Subject: your October case study\n>\n> Saw the retail piece you published last week — the before/after structure is the clearest I have read this year.\n>\n> We built something that produces that structure\'s first draft from a client interview transcript. Agencies using it cut drafting from a day to about forty minutes; the editing judgement stays yours.\n>\n> Worth fifteen minutes?\n\nOne specific observation, one concrete number, one small ask. The observation has to be real — that is the part that cannot be templated.','gpt-5.6-terra',106,144,1.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-17 09:17:01'),(119,36,'user','What is a reasonable follow-up if they do not reply?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-17 09:18:00'),(120,36,'assistant','One follow-up, five business days later, shorter than the first and adding something rather than asking again:\n\n> Sent this over last week. Since then we published the transcript-to-draft walkthrough — here it is, in case it is more useful than a call.\n\nThen stop. A third email converts almost nobody and costs you the address.','gpt-5.6-terra',313,82,1.5800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-17 09:18:58'),(121,37,'user','I have a 40-page quarterly report. What is the best way to get a usable summary out of it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 18:59:00'),(122,37,'assistant','Do not ask for \"a summary\" — ask for the shape you need. Three that work:\n\n1. **Decision brief**: what changed, what it means, what someone must now decide. Half a page.\n2. **Number sheet**: every figure that moved more than 10%, with its prior value.\n3. **Exception list**: only what contradicts last quarter\'s expectations.\n\nGenerate all three from the same document and read the exception list first — that is where anything worth acting on hides.','grok-4.5',103,113,0.4300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 19:00:20'),(123,38,'user','A competitor launched at half our price and we are losing deals. How should we respond?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 07:09:00'),(124,38,'assistant','Do not match the price; you cannot win a race you started second in.\n\nFind the customers for whom the cheaper product is expensive — the ones whose cost of a bad output is high (regulated copy, client deliverables, anything with a legal review downstream). Reposition around that risk, not around features.\n\nConcretely: change the comparison page from a feature grid to a \"what happens when it is wrong\" section. Buyers who ignore that section were never going to pay your price anyway.','gemini-3.5-flash',102,122,0.6700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 07:09:34'),(125,38,'user','Should sales be allowed to discount to close the gap?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 07:11:00'),(126,38,'assistant','Give them one lever, not a range: a fixed 15% for annual prepay, nothing else. An open discount ceiling teaches the market your list price is fiction, and the reps discover the floor within a quarter.','gemini-3.5-flash',314,50,1.0900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-18 07:12:05'),(127,39,'user','Our landing page converts at 0.8%. The hero says \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". What is wrong?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 11:53:00'),(128,39,'assistant','The hero makes no claim a visitor can test. \"The future of content\" describes a category, not what happens if they sign up.\n\nReplace it with the outcome and the constraint: \"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\" Then put the proof directly under it, not three sections down.\n\n0.8% on a page with a category-level headline is normal. Fixing the headline usually moves it more than any redesign will.','gpt-5.6-terra',106,113,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 11:54:08'),(129,40,'user','Turn our post about AI writing workflows into a LinkedIn post that does not read like an ad.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 16:24:00'),(130,40,'assistant','> We measured where the hour actually goes when our team writes an article.\n>\n> Drafting: 12 minutes. Research: 20. Deciding what the piece is about: 28.\n>\n> We had automated the 12.\n>\n> Everything we have shipped since then targets the 28 — outline pressure-testing, angle comparison, source triage. It is less impressive in a demo and considerably more useful.\n\nOne surprising number, one admission, one implication. No CTA — comments do that work for you on LinkedIn.','gemini-3.5-flash',103,118,0.6600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 16:24:25'),(131,40,'user','Give me three hooks I could A/B against that opener.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 16:27:00'),(132,40,'assistant','1. \"Automating the writing was the easy part. It was also the wrong part.\"\n2. \"We timed every step of writing one article. The result changed our roadmap.\"\n3. \"Twelve minutes of drafting. Twenty-eight minutes of deciding. Guess which one we automated first.\"\n\nThe third tends to win because it makes the reader answer before they scroll.','gemini-3.5-flash',313,85,1.1900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-19 16:28:27'),(133,41,'user','Plan two weeks of social posts leading into a launch. B2B SaaS, small audience.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 18:39:00'),(134,41,'assistant','Week one, build the problem: three posts on the pain, each with a number or a story, none mentioning the product. One customer quote. One \"here is how we work today\" behind-the-scenes.\n\nWeek two, close the loop: teaser with a real screenshot (day 8), the reasoning behind one design decision (day 10), launch (day 12), then two days of answering replies in public rather than posting anything new.\n\nWith a small audience the replies matter more than the posts. Budget time for them.','gpt-5.4-mini',100,121,0.4400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 18:40:18'),(135,42,'user','Monthly churn jumped from 3.1% to 5.4%. Where do I look first?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 09:54:00'),(136,42,'assistant','Segment before you theorise. Split the churned accounts by:\n\n- **Tenure** — a spike concentrated in month-two accounts is onboarding; spread evenly across tenures is pricing or a competitor.\n- **Plan** — if it is one tier, look at what changed in that tier\'s limits.\n- **Acquisition channel** — a bad cohort from one campaign can move the whole number.\n\nOne of those three almost always isolates it. If none do, check for an involuntary-churn spike: expired cards and failed renewals show up as churn but are a billing problem.','gpt-5.6-terra',96,132,0.9100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 09:55:06'),(137,42,'user','It is concentrated in month two on the mid tier. What now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 09:57:00'),(138,42,'assistant','That is an expectation gap, not a product gap: they bought something in month one and did not find it by month two.\n\nPull the last 20 churned mid-tier accounts and check which features they never touched. If a feature that justifies the tier\'s price went unused in 15 of 20, the onboarding never routed them to it — that is a sequencing fix, not a discount.','gpt-5.6-terra',315,90,1.6200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 09:58:07'),(139,43,'user','Credits versus flat unlimited for an AI product — which should we pick?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 16:28:00'),(140,43,'assistant','They fail differently, and you should pick the failure you can survive.\n\n**Credits** align revenue with cost, so a heavy user cannot bankrupt you — but they add friction to every action and buyers hate metering they cannot predict.\n\n**Flat unlimited** sells better and forecasts worse; your margin is hostage to your top 5% of users, and one automation-happy customer can eat a tier\'s profit.\n\nMost products land on flat pricing with a fair-use ceiling nobody normal reaches. That gets the sales advantage while keeping the escape hatch.','deepseek-v4-pro',98,135,0.2300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-20 16:28:52'),(141,44,'user','Our dashboard looks dead for new users. What should empty states actually do?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-21 11:26:00'),(142,44,'assistant','An empty state has one job: make the next action obvious and small.\n\nDrop the illustration-plus-\"Nothing here yet\" pattern. Instead show the panel as it will look with data, greyed at 40% opacity, with one button that produces exactly that. People understand a preview instantly and a description never.\n\nIf the action needs setup, show the count of steps left, not a wall of prose.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,96,0.7800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-21 11:27:18'),(143,44,'user','How many actions should the empty dashboard offer?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-21 11:31:00'),(144,44,'assistant','One primary, one escape hatch. \"Generate your first article\" plus a quiet \"Import existing content\" link. Three equal buttons is a menu, and a menu on an empty screen is a decision the user is not equipped to make yet.','gpt-5.6-terra',313,55,1.4700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-21 11:31:28'),(145,45,'user','How do I check whether our brand blue works for body text?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 09:33:00'),(146,45,'assistant','Contrast ratio against the background it sits on: 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for text above ~24px or bold above ~19px, and 3:1 for the non-text parts of a control such as an input border.\n\nBrand blues usually fail on white for body copy and pass for headings. The fix is not abandoning the colour — it is keeping the brand blue for large text and interactive fills, and using a darkened variant for anything paragraph-sized.','claude-sonnet-4-6',95,106,0.8000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 09:34:03'),(147,46,'user','What should I actually record when scanning competitor features?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 08:16:00'),(148,46,'assistant','Record four columns and ignore everything else: the feature, the tier it is gated behind, when it shipped, and whether their marketing leads with it.\n\nThe gating and the date are the informative pair. A feature that shipped 18 months ago and is still enterprise-only is a moat; one that moved down to the entry tier last quarter is table stakes you now have to match.','gpt-5.4-mini',96,92,0.3800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 08:16:34'),(149,46,'user','How often is it worth redoing this?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 08:20:00'),(150,46,'assistant','Quarterly for the full scan, with changelog subscriptions in between. Monthly scans mostly capture noise, and anything faster than that becomes a report nobody reads.','gpt-5.4-mini',309,42,0.7000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-22 08:20:37'),(151,47,'user','I have five features requested by customers and time for one. How do I choose?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 18:55:00'),(152,47,'assistant','Ask what each one is evidence of rather than how many people asked.\n\nFor each request, write down: which customer segment asked, what they were trying to do when they hit the wall, and what they are doing instead today. Requests with a workaround already in place are lower urgency than they sound — the pain is real but survivable.\n\nThen pick the one where the workaround is worst, not the one with the most votes. Vote counts measure how many people bothered to ask, which correlates with how loud a segment is, not how much it matters.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,135,0.9400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 18:55:43'),(153,47,'user','Two of them tie on that test. Tiebreaker?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 18:58:00'),(154,47,'assistant','Build the one that teaches you more. At a tie on customer value, the option that resolves a question about your product\'s direction is worth more than the one that confirms what you already believe — you will make the next five decisions with what it tells you.','gpt-5.6-terra',311,66,1.5100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 18:58:37'),(155,48,'user','Write me an image prompt for a hero banner for an AI writing tool.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 13:15:00'),(156,48,'assistant','> Wide editorial hero image: a clean desk at dawn, a single open laptop with a soft-glowing screen, warm side light from a window on the left, muted navy and amber palette, shallow depth of field, generous empty space on the right for headline text, photographic, no text, no logos, no hands.\n\nThe two clauses doing the real work are the empty space on the right and \"no text\" — models will happily fill your headline area with garbled lettering otherwise.','gpt-5.6-terra',97,114,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 13:16:07'),(157,49,'user','There are a dozen models in the picker. How do I choose one?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 17:34:00'),(158,49,'assistant','Pick by task shape, not by benchmark:\n\n- **Long reasoning, code, anything you will ship** — the largest model you can afford; the credit difference is smaller than the time you lose re-checking a cheap answer.\n- **Bulk work — captions, tags, rewrites** — a fast, cheap model. Quality differences vanish at short output lengths.\n- **Anything needing current information** — a search-backed model, or the answer will be confidently out of date.\n\nWhen unsure, run the same prompt through a cheap and an expensive model once. The gap on your work is usually different from the gap on the leaderboard.','gpt-5.4-mini',95,149,0.4900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-23 17:34:28'),(159,50,'user','My queued jobs are marked failed but nothing lands in the failed_jobs table. Laravel 12, Redis driver.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 14:53:00'),(160,50,'assistant','Almost always one of three things:\n\n1. The worker was started before the failed-job table existed — `php artisan queue:failed-table` then migrate, and restart the worker.\n2. `queue.failed.driver` is set to `null` in config (common in a copied .env).\n3. The job throws in `__construct`, which happens on dispatch, not in the worker — that exception goes to your normal log, not the failed table.\n\nCheck `config(\'queue.failed\')` first; it is the cheapest of the three to rule out.','gpt-5.6-terra',106,120,0.9000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 14:53:51'),(161,50,'user','It was the null driver. How do I get an alert when a job fails now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 14:58:00'),(162,50,'assistant','Hook the `JobFailed` event in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`:\n\n```php\nQueue::failing(function (JobFailed $event) {\n    Log::critical(\'Job failed\', [\n        \'connection\' => $event->connectionName,\n        \'job\' => $event->job->resolveName(),\n        \'exception\' => $event->exception->getMessage(),\n    ]);\n});\n```\n\nPoint that log channel at Slack or your error tracker rather than building a notification path of your own.','gpt-5.6-terra',317,105,1.6900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 14:59:28'),(163,50,'user','Should I set tries or backoff on the job class?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 15:03:00'),(164,50,'assistant','Set both, and set them per job rather than globally. `public $tries = 3;` with `public array $backoff = [10, 60, 300];` gives you three attempts spread over roughly six minutes, which covers a provider blipping without hammering it. For anything that charges money on each attempt, use `$tries = 1` and requeue deliberately.','gpt-5.6-terra',532,81,2.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 15:03:38'),(165,51,'user','I have a 600-line controller handling checkout. Where do I start splitting it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 07:21:00'),(166,51,'assistant','Split by reason to change, not by line count.\n\nStart with the three things a checkout controller usually conflates: request validation (move to a FormRequest), the payment call and its failure handling (a service), and the post-payment side effects — invoice, email, credits (listeners on one event). What is left in the controller should read as five or six lines you can take in at a glance.\n\nDo the FormRequest first: it is the lowest-risk move and it usually deletes 100 lines on its own.','deepseek-v4-pro',100,123,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 07:22:26'),(167,51,'user','How do I keep the refactor safe without a full test suite?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 07:23:00'),(168,51,'assistant','Write one characterisation test per branch you are about to move — it does not have to be pretty, only faithful to current behaviour. Then move code in commits small enough that reverting one is painless. Resist renaming anything in the same commit as a move; a diff that both moves and renames is unreviewable.','deepseek-v4-pro',315,78,0.3900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 07:23:33'),(169,52,'user','What does /^(?!.*\\.\\.)[\\w.-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i actually match?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 16:43:00'),(170,52,'assistant','Piece by piece:\n\n- `(?!.*\\.\\.)` — a negative lookahead rejecting anything containing two consecutive dots.\n- `[\\w.-]+` — the local part: letters, digits, underscore, dot, hyphen.\n- `@[\\w-]+` — a domain label without dots.\n- `\\.[a-z]{2,}` — a dot and a TLD of two or more letters.\n- `/i` — case-insensitive.\n\nSo it matches `first.last@example.com` but not `a..b@example.com` or `user@sub.example.com` — note the single-label domain, which is probably a bug for you.','gpt-5.6-terra',97,116,0.8500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-24 16:43:43'),(171,53,'user','Rewrite this so it is shorter and less breathless: \"We are incredibly excited to finally announce the launch of our brand new AI-powered platform which we have been working on for many months.\"',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 13:15:00'),(172,53,'assistant','\"Our AI platform is live today.\"\n\nThat is the whole sentence. Excitement belongs in what the product does next, not in an adverb before it — readers discount \"incredibly excited\" automatically, and \"finally\" tells them you were late.\n\nIf you want one line of context: \"Our AI platform is live today, after eight months in private beta with 40 teams.\" The number does the work the adjectives were trying to do.','gpt-5.6-terra',129,103,0.9300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 13:16:29'),(173,53,'user','Now give me the opening paragraph that follows it.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 13:19:00'),(174,53,'assistant','\"It does one thing: turns the research you already have into publishable drafts, in your voice, in about a minute. No prompt engineering, no blank page. Paste your notes, pick a format, edit what comes back.\"\n\nThree sentences, one claim each — capability, cost, action. Everything else you want to say belongs further down, where the readers who are still with you will find it.','gpt-5.6-terra',313,95,1.6300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 13:20:04'),(175,54,'user','Write a cold email to agency owners about our AI content tool. Every version I write sounds like spam.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 07:58:00'),(176,54,'assistant','It sounds like spam because it opens with you. Try:\n\n> Subject: your October case study\n>\n> Saw the retail piece you published last week — the before/after structure is the clearest I have read this year.\n>\n> We built something that produces that structure\'s first draft from a client interview transcript. Agencies using it cut drafting from a day to about forty minutes; the editing judgement stays yours.\n>\n> Worth fifteen minutes?\n\nOne specific observation, one concrete number, one small ask. The observation has to be real — that is the part that cannot be templated.','claude-sonnet-4-6',106,144,1.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 07:58:30'),(177,55,'user','I have a 40-page quarterly report. What is the best way to get a usable summary out of it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 15:08:00'),(178,55,'assistant','Do not ask for \"a summary\" — ask for the shape you need. Three that work:\n\n1. **Decision brief**: what changed, what it means, what someone must now decide. Half a page.\n2. **Number sheet**: every figure that moved more than 10%, with its prior value.\n3. **Exception list**: only what contradicts last quarter\'s expectations.\n\nGenerate all three from the same document and read the exception list first — that is where anything worth acting on hides.','deepseek-v4-pro',103,113,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 15:09:02'),(179,56,'user','A competitor launched at half our price and we are losing deals. How should we respond?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 10:00:00'),(180,56,'assistant','Do not match the price; you cannot win a race you started second in.\n\nFind the customers for whom the cheaper product is expensive — the ones whose cost of a bad output is high (regulated copy, client deliverables, anything with a legal review downstream). Reposition around that risk, not around features.\n\nConcretely: change the comparison page from a feature grid to a \"what happens when it is wrong\" section. Buyers who ignore that section were never going to pay your price anyway.','deepseek-v4-pro',102,122,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 10:01:25'),(181,56,'user','Should sales be allowed to discount to close the gap?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 10:02:00'),(182,56,'assistant','Give them one lever, not a range: a fixed 15% for annual prepay, nothing else. An open discount ceiling teaches the market your list price is fiction, and the reps discover the floor within a quarter.','deepseek-v4-pro',314,50,0.3600,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 10:03:22'),(183,57,'user','Our landing page converts at 0.8%. The hero says \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". What is wrong?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 19:56:00'),(184,57,'assistant','The hero makes no claim a visitor can test. \"The future of content\" describes a category, not what happens if they sign up.\n\nReplace it with the outcome and the constraint: \"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\" Then put the proof directly under it, not three sections down.\n\n0.8% on a page with a category-level headline is normal. Fixing the headline usually moves it more than any redesign will.','claude-sonnet-4-6',106,113,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 19:56:39'),(185,58,'user','Turn our post about AI writing workflows into a LinkedIn post that does not read like an ad.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 11:07:00'),(186,58,'assistant','> We measured where the hour actually goes when our team writes an article.\n>\n> Drafting: 12 minutes. Research: 20. Deciding what the piece is about: 28.\n>\n> We had automated the 12.\n>\n> Everything we have shipped since then targets the 28 — outline pressure-testing, angle comparison, source triage. It is less impressive in a demo and considerably more useful.\n\nOne surprising number, one admission, one implication. No CTA — comments do that work for you on LinkedIn.','gpt-5.6-terra',103,118,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 11:08:07'),(187,58,'user','Give me three hooks I could A/B against that opener.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 11:09:00'),(188,58,'assistant','1. \"Automating the writing was the easy part. It was also the wrong part.\"\n2. \"We timed every step of writing one article. The result changed our roadmap.\"\n3. \"Twelve minutes of drafting. Twenty-eight minutes of deciding. Guess which one we automated first.\"\n\nThe third tends to win because it makes the reader answer before they scroll.','gpt-5.6-terra',313,85,1.5900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-26 11:09:26'),(189,59,'user','Plan two weeks of social posts leading into a launch. B2B SaaS, small audience.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 08:51:00'),(190,59,'assistant','Week one, build the problem: three posts on the pain, each with a number or a story, none mentioning the product. One customer quote. One \"here is how we work today\" behind-the-scenes.\n\nWeek two, close the loop: teaser with a real screenshot (day 8), the reasoning behind one design decision (day 10), launch (day 12), then two days of answering replies in public rather than posting anything new.\n\nWith a small audience the replies matter more than the posts. Budget time for them.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,121,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 08:51:25'),(191,60,'user','Monthly churn jumped from 3.1% to 5.4%. Where do I look first?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 17:41:00'),(192,60,'assistant','Segment before you theorise. Split the churned accounts by:\n\n- **Tenure** — a spike concentrated in month-two accounts is onboarding; spread evenly across tenures is pricing or a competitor.\n- **Plan** — if it is one tier, look at what changed in that tier\'s limits.\n- **Acquisition channel** — a bad cohort from one campaign can move the whole number.\n\nOne of those three almost always isolates it. If none do, check for an involuntary-churn spike: expired cards and failed renewals show up as churn but are a billing problem.','gpt-5.6-terra',96,132,0.9100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 17:42:21'),(193,60,'user','It is concentrated in month two on the mid tier. What now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 17:43:00'),(194,60,'assistant','That is an expectation gap, not a product gap: they bought something in month one and did not find it by month two.\n\nPull the last 20 churned mid-tier accounts and check which features they never touched. If a feature that justifies the tier\'s price went unused in 15 of 20, the onboarding never routed them to it — that is a sequencing fix, not a discount.','gpt-5.6-terra',315,90,1.6200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-27 17:44:21'),(195,61,'user','Credits versus flat unlimited for an AI product — which should we pick?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 19:30:00'),(196,61,'assistant','They fail differently, and you should pick the failure you can survive.\n\n**Credits** align revenue with cost, so a heavy user cannot bankrupt you — but they add friction to every action and buyers hate metering they cannot predict.\n\n**Flat unlimited** sells better and forecasts worse; your margin is hostage to your top 5% of users, and one automation-happy customer can eat a tier\'s profit.\n\nMost products land on flat pricing with a fair-use ceiling nobody normal reaches. That gets the sales advantage while keeping the escape hatch.','gpt-5.4-mini',98,135,0.4700,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 19:30:24'),(197,62,'user','Our dashboard looks dead for new users. What should empty states actually do?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 17:55:00'),(198,62,'assistant','An empty state has one job: make the next action obvious and small.\n\nDrop the illustration-plus-\"Nothing here yet\" pattern. Instead show the panel as it will look with data, greyed at 40% opacity, with one button that produces exactly that. People understand a preview instantly and a description never.\n\nIf the action needs setup, show the count of steps left, not a wall of prose.','gpt-5.4-mini',100,96,0.3900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 17:56:25'),(199,63,'user','How do I check whether our brand blue works for body text?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 14:03:00'),(200,63,'assistant','Contrast ratio against the background it sits on: 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for text above ~24px or bold above ~19px, and 3:1 for the non-text parts of a control such as an input border.\n\nBrand blues usually fail on white for body copy and pass for headings. The fix is not abandoning the colour — it is keeping the brand blue for large text and interactive fills, and using a darkened variant for anything paragraph-sized.','claude-sonnet-4-6',95,106,0.8000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-28 14:04:22'),(201,64,'user','What should I actually record when scanning competitor features?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 07:54:00'),(202,64,'assistant','Record four columns and ignore everything else: the feature, the tier it is gated behind, when it shipped, and whether their marketing leads with it.\n\nThe gating and the date are the informative pair. A feature that shipped 18 months ago and is still enterprise-only is a moat; one that moved down to the entry tier last quarter is table stakes you now have to match.','deepseek-v4-pro',96,92,0.1900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 07:55:03'),(203,64,'user','How often is it worth redoing this?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 07:56:00'),(204,64,'assistant','Quarterly for the full scan, with changelog subscriptions in between. Monthly scans mostly capture noise, and anything faster than that becomes a report nobody reads.','deepseek-v4-pro',309,42,0.3500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 07:57:11'),(205,65,'user','I have five features requested by customers and time for one. How do I choose?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 16:29:00'),(206,65,'assistant','Ask what each one is evidence of rather than how many people asked.\n\nFor each request, write down: which customer segment asked, what they were trying to do when they hit the wall, and what they are doing instead today. Requests with a workaround already in place are lower urgency than they sound — the pain is real but survivable.\n\nThen pick the one where the workaround is worst, not the one with the most votes. Vote counts measure how many people bothered to ask, which correlates with how loud a segment is, not how much it matters.','gemini-3.5-flash',100,135,0.7100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 16:29:51'),(207,65,'user','Two of them tie on that test. Tiebreaker?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 16:32:00'),(208,65,'assistant','Build the one that teaches you more. At a tie on customer value, the option that resolves a question about your product\'s direction is worth more than the one that confirms what you already believe — you will make the next five decisions with what it tells you.','gemini-3.5-flash',311,66,1.1300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 16:33:15'),(209,66,'user','Write me an image prompt for a hero banner for an AI writing tool.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:49:00'),(210,66,'assistant','> Wide editorial hero image: a clean desk at dawn, a single open laptop with a soft-glowing screen, warm side light from a window on the left, muted navy and amber palette, shallow depth of field, generous empty space on the right for headline text, photographic, no text, no logos, no hands.\n\nThe two clauses doing the real work are the empty space on the right and \"no text\" — models will happily fill your headline area with garbled lettering otherwise.','gpt-5.6-terra',97,114,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 09:50:29'),(211,67,'user','There are a dozen models in the picker. How do I choose one?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 17:47:00'),(212,67,'assistant','Pick by task shape, not by benchmark:\n\n- **Long reasoning, code, anything you will ship** — the largest model you can afford; the credit difference is smaller than the time you lose re-checking a cheap answer.\n- **Bulk work — captions, tags, rewrites** — a fast, cheap model. Quality differences vanish at short output lengths.\n- **Anything needing current information** — a search-backed model, or the answer will be confidently out of date.\n\nWhen unsure, run the same prompt through a cheap and an expensive model once. The gap on your work is usually different from the gap on the leaderboard.','gpt-5.4-mini',95,149,0.4900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 17:47:42'),(213,68,'user','My queued jobs are marked failed but nothing lands in the failed_jobs table. Laravel 12, Redis driver.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 20:53:00'),(214,68,'assistant','Almost always one of three things:\n\n1. The worker was started before the failed-job table existed — `php artisan queue:failed-table` then migrate, and restart the worker.\n2. `queue.failed.driver` is set to `null` in config (common in a copied .env).\n3. The job throws in `__construct`, which happens on dispatch, not in the worker — that exception goes to your normal log, not the failed table.\n\nCheck `config(\'queue.failed\')` first; it is the cheapest of the three to rule out.','claude-haiku-4-5',106,120,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 20:53:27'),(215,68,'user','It was the null driver. How do I get an alert when a job fails now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 20:56:00'),(216,68,'assistant','Hook the `JobFailed` event in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`:\n\n```php\nQueue::failing(function (JobFailed $event) {\n    Log::critical(\'Job failed\', [\n        \'connection\' => $event->connectionName,\n        \'job\' => $event->job->resolveName(),\n        \'exception\' => $event->exception->getMessage(),\n    ]);\n});\n```\n\nPoint that log channel at Slack or your error tracker rather than building a notification path of your own.','claude-haiku-4-5',317,105,0.8400,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 20:57:13'),(217,68,'user','Should I set tries or backoff on the job class?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 21:01:00'),(218,68,'assistant','Set both, and set them per job rather than globally. `public $tries = 3;` with `public array $backoff = [10, 60, 300];` gives you three attempts spread over roughly six minutes, which covers a provider blipping without hammering it. For anything that charges money on each attempt, use `$tries = 1` and requeue deliberately.','claude-haiku-4-5',532,81,1.2300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 21:02:15'),(219,69,'user','I have a 600-line controller handling checkout. Where do I start splitting it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 13:31:00'),(220,69,'assistant','Split by reason to change, not by line count.\n\nStart with the three things a checkout controller usually conflates: request validation (move to a FormRequest), the payment call and its failure handling (a service), and the post-payment side effects — invoice, email, credits (listeners on one event). What is left in the controller should read as five or six lines you can take in at a glance.\n\nDo the FormRequest first: it is the lowest-risk move and it usually deletes 100 lines on its own.','claude-haiku-4-5',100,123,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 13:31:45'),(221,69,'user','How do I keep the refactor safe without a full test suite?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 13:35:00'),(222,69,'assistant','Write one characterisation test per branch you are about to move — it does not have to be pretty, only faithful to current behaviour. Then move code in commits small enough that reverting one is painless. Resist renaming anything in the same commit as a move; a diff that both moves and renames is unreviewable.','claude-haiku-4-5',315,78,0.7900,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-30 13:36:26'),(223,70,'user','What does /^(?!.*\\.\\.)[\\w.-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i actually match?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 13:23:00'),(224,70,'assistant','Piece by piece:\n\n- `(?!.*\\.\\.)` — a negative lookahead rejecting anything containing two consecutive dots.\n- `[\\w.-]+` — the local part: letters, digits, underscore, dot, hyphen.\n- `@[\\w-]+` — a domain label without dots.\n- `\\.[a-z]{2,}` — a dot and a TLD of two or more letters.\n- `/i` — case-insensitive.\n\nSo it matches `first.last@example.com` but not `a..b@example.com` or `user@sub.example.com` — note the single-label domain, which is probably a bug for you.','gpt-5.4-mini',97,116,0.4300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 13:24:01'),(225,71,'user','Rewrite this so it is shorter and less breathless: \"We are incredibly excited to finally announce the launch of our brand new AI-powered platform which we have been working on for many months.\"',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 09:27:00'),(226,71,'assistant','\"Our AI platform is live today.\"\n\nThat is the whole sentence. Excitement belongs in what the product does next, not in an adverb before it — readers discount \"incredibly excited\" automatically, and \"finally\" tells them you were late.\n\nIf you want one line of context: \"Our AI platform is live today, after eight months in private beta with 40 teams.\" The number does the work the adjectives were trying to do.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',129,103,0.2300,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 09:27:31'),(227,71,'user','Now give me the opening paragraph that follows it.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 09:32:00'),(228,71,'assistant','\"It does one thing: turns the research you already have into publishable drafts, in your voice, in about a minute. No prompt engineering, no blank page. Paste your notes, pick a format, edit what comes back.\"\n\nThree sentences, one claim each — capability, cost, action. Everything else you want to say belongs further down, where the readers who are still with you will find it.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',313,95,0.4100,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 09:32:49'),(229,72,'user','Write a cold email to agency owners about our AI content tool. Every version I write sounds like spam.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 19:22:00'),(230,72,'assistant','It sounds like spam because it opens with you. Try:\n\n> Subject: your October case study\n>\n> Saw the retail piece you published last week — the before/after structure is the clearest I have read this year.\n>\n> We built something that produces that structure\'s first draft from a client interview transcript. Agencies using it cut drafting from a day to about forty minutes; the editing judgement stays yours.\n>\n> Worth fifteen minutes?\n\nOne specific observation, one concrete number, one small ask. The observation has to be real — that is the part that cannot be templated.','gpt-5.6-terra',106,144,1.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 19:23:21'),(231,72,'user','What is a reasonable follow-up if they do not reply?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 19:24:00'),(232,72,'assistant','One follow-up, five business days later, shorter than the first and adding something rather than asking again:\n\n> Sent this over last week. Since then we published the transcript-to-draft walkthrough — here it is, in case it is more useful than a call.\n\nThen stop. A third email converts almost nobody and costs you the address.','gpt-5.6-terra',313,82,1.5800,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 19:25:28'),(233,73,'user','I have a 40-page quarterly report. What is the best way to get a usable summary out of it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 10:43:00'),(234,73,'assistant','Do not ask for \"a summary\" — ask for the shape you need. Three that work:\n\n1. **Decision brief**: what changed, what it means, what someone must now decide. Half a page.\n2. **Number sheet**: every figure that moved more than 10%, with its prior value.\n3. **Exception list**: only what contradicts last quarter\'s expectations.\n\nGenerate all three from the same document and read the exception list first — that is where anything worth acting on hides.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',103,113,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-31 10:43:33'),(235,74,'user','A competitor launched at half our price and we are losing deals. How should we respond?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:51:00'),(236,74,'assistant','Do not match the price; you cannot win a race you started second in.\n\nFind the customers for whom the cheaper product is expensive — the ones whose cost of a bad output is high (regulated copy, client deliverables, anything with a legal review downstream). Reposition around that risk, not around features.\n\nConcretely: change the comparison page from a feature grid to a \"what happens when it is wrong\" section. Buyers who ignore that section were never going to pay your price anyway.','gpt-5.4-mini',102,122,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:52:06'),(237,74,'user','Should sales be allowed to discount to close the gap?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:55:00'),(238,74,'assistant','Give them one lever, not a range: a fixed 15% for annual prepay, nothing else. An open discount ceiling teaches the market your list price is fiction, and the reps discover the floor within a quarter.','gpt-5.4-mini',314,50,0.7300,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:56:11'),(239,75,'user','Our landing page converts at 0.8%. The hero says \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". What is wrong?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 14:48:00'),(240,75,'assistant','The hero makes no claim a visitor can test. \"The future of content\" describes a category, not what happens if they sign up.\n\nReplace it with the outcome and the constraint: \"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\" Then put the proof directly under it, not three sections down.\n\n0.8% on a page with a category-level headline is normal. Fixing the headline usually moves it more than any redesign will.','gemini-3.5-flash',106,113,0.6600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 14:48:35'),(241,76,'user','Turn our post about AI writing workflows into a LinkedIn post that does not read like an ad.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 17:59:00'),(242,76,'assistant','> We measured where the hour actually goes when our team writes an article.\n>\n> Drafting: 12 minutes. Research: 20. Deciding what the piece is about: 28.\n>\n> We had automated the 12.\n>\n> Everything we have shipped since then targets the 28 — outline pressure-testing, angle comparison, source triage. It is less impressive in a demo and considerably more useful.\n\nOne surprising number, one admission, one implication. No CTA — comments do that work for you on LinkedIn.','claude-sonnet-4-6',103,118,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 18:00:16'),(243,76,'user','Give me three hooks I could A/B against that opener.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 18:02:00'),(244,76,'assistant','1. \"Automating the writing was the easy part. It was also the wrong part.\"\n2. \"We timed every step of writing one article. The result changed our roadmap.\"\n3. \"Twelve minutes of drafting. Twenty-eight minutes of deciding. Guess which one we automated first.\"\n\nThe third tends to win because it makes the reader answer before they scroll.','claude-sonnet-4-6',313,85,1.5900,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 18:03:03'),(245,77,'user','Plan two weeks of social posts leading into a launch. B2B SaaS, small audience.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:20:00'),(246,77,'assistant','Week one, build the problem: three posts on the pain, each with a number or a story, none mentioning the product. One customer quote. One \"here is how we work today\" behind-the-scenes.\n\nWeek two, close the loop: teaser with a real screenshot (day 8), the reasoning behind one design decision (day 10), launch (day 12), then two days of answering replies in public rather than posting anything new.\n\nWith a small audience the replies matter more than the posts. Budget time for them.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,121,0.8800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-01 15:21:24'),(247,78,'user','Monthly churn jumped from 3.1% to 5.4%. Where do I look first?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:19:00'),(248,78,'assistant','Segment before you theorise. Split the churned accounts by:\n\n- **Tenure** — a spike concentrated in month-two accounts is onboarding; spread evenly across tenures is pricing or a competitor.\n- **Plan** — if it is one tier, look at what changed in that tier\'s limits.\n- **Acquisition channel** — a bad cohort from one campaign can move the whole number.\n\nOne of those three almost always isolates it. If none do, check for an involuntary-churn spike: expired cards and failed renewals show up as churn but are a billing problem.','claude-haiku-4-5',96,132,0.4600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:20:29'),(249,78,'user','It is concentrated in month two on the mid tier. What now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:24:00'),(250,78,'assistant','That is an expectation gap, not a product gap: they bought something in month one and did not find it by month two.\n\nPull the last 20 churned mid-tier accounts and check which features they never touched. If a feature that justifies the tier\'s price went unused in 15 of 20, the onboarding never routed them to it — that is a sequencing fix, not a discount.','claude-haiku-4-5',315,90,0.8100,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:25:18'),(251,79,'user','Credits versus flat unlimited for an AI product — which should we pick?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 13:37:00'),(252,79,'assistant','They fail differently, and you should pick the failure you can survive.\n\n**Credits** align revenue with cost, so a heavy user cannot bankrupt you — but they add friction to every action and buyers hate metering they cannot predict.\n\n**Flat unlimited** sells better and forecasts worse; your margin is hostage to your top 5% of users, and one automation-happy customer can eat a tier\'s profit.\n\nMost products land on flat pricing with a fair-use ceiling nobody normal reaches. That gets the sales advantage while keeping the escape hatch.','gpt-5.6-terra',98,135,0.9300,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 13:38:13'),(253,80,'user','Our dashboard looks dead for new users. What should empty states actually do?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:45:00'),(254,80,'assistant','An empty state has one job: make the next action obvious and small.\n\nDrop the illustration-plus-\"Nothing here yet\" pattern. Instead show the panel as it will look with data, greyed at 40% opacity, with one button that produces exactly that. People understand a preview instantly and a description never.\n\nIf the action needs setup, show the count of steps left, not a wall of prose.','deepseek-v4-pro',100,96,0.2000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:45:37'),(255,80,'user','How many actions should the empty dashboard offer?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:48:00'),(256,80,'assistant','One primary, one escape hatch. \"Generate your first article\" plus a quiet \"Import existing content\" link. Three equal buttons is a menu, and a menu on an empty screen is a decision the user is not equipped to make yet.','deepseek-v4-pro',313,55,0.3700,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:48:39'),(257,81,'user','How do I check whether our brand blue works for body text?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:52:00'),(258,81,'assistant','Contrast ratio against the background it sits on: 4.5:1 for body text, 3:1 for text above ~24px or bold above ~19px, and 3:1 for the non-text parts of a control such as an input border.\n\nBrand blues usually fail on white for body copy and pass for headings. The fix is not abandoning the colour — it is keeping the brand blue for large text and interactive fills, and using a darkened variant for anything paragraph-sized.','claude-sonnet-4-6',95,106,0.8000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 16:52:45'),(259,82,'user','What should I actually record when scanning competitor features?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:17:00'),(260,82,'assistant','Record four columns and ignore everything else: the feature, the tier it is gated behind, when it shipped, and whether their marketing leads with it.\n\nThe gating and the date are the informative pair. A feature that shipped 18 months ago and is still enterprise-only is a moat; one that moved down to the entry tier last quarter is table stakes you now have to match.','gpt-5.4-mini',96,92,0.3800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:18:11'),(261,82,'user','How often is it worth redoing this?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:21:00'),(262,82,'assistant','Quarterly for the full scan, with changelog subscriptions in between. Monthly scans mostly capture noise, and anything faster than that becomes a report nobody reads.','gpt-5.4-mini',309,42,0.7000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-02 09:22:10'),(263,83,'user','I have five features requested by customers and time for one. How do I choose?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 14:21:00'),(264,83,'assistant','Ask what each one is evidence of rather than how many people asked.\n\nFor each request, write down: which customer segment asked, what they were trying to do when they hit the wall, and what they are doing instead today. Requests with a workaround already in place are lower urgency than they sound — the pain is real but survivable.\n\nThen pick the one where the workaround is worst, not the one with the most votes. Vote counts measure how many people bothered to ask, which correlates with how loud a segment is, not how much it matters.','gpt-5.6-terra',100,135,0.9400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 14:21:29'),(265,84,'user','Write me an image prompt for a hero banner for an AI writing tool.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 20:07:00'),(266,84,'assistant','> Wide editorial hero image: a clean desk at dawn, a single open laptop with a soft-glowing screen, warm side light from a window on the left, muted navy and amber palette, shallow depth of field, generous empty space on the right for headline text, photographic, no text, no logos, no hands.\n\nThe two clauses doing the real work are the empty space on the right and \"no text\" — models will happily fill your headline area with garbled lettering otherwise.','gpt-5.4-mini',97,114,0.4200,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 20:07:42'),(267,85,'user','There are a dozen models in the picker. How do I choose one?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 16:13:00'),(268,85,'assistant','Pick by task shape, not by benchmark:\n\n- **Long reasoning, code, anything you will ship** — the largest model you can afford; the credit difference is smaller than the time you lose re-checking a cheap answer.\n- **Bulk work — captions, tags, rewrites** — a fast, cheap model. Quality differences vanish at short output lengths.\n- **Anything needing current information** — a search-backed model, or the answer will be confidently out of date.\n\nWhen unsure, run the same prompt through a cheap and an expensive model once. The gap on your work is usually different from the gap on the leaderboard.','claude-sonnet-4-6',95,149,0.9800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 16:14:04'),(269,86,'user','My queued jobs are marked failed but nothing lands in the failed_jobs table. Laravel 12, Redis driver.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:31:00'),(270,86,'assistant','Almost always one of three things:\n\n1. The worker was started before the failed-job table existed — `php artisan queue:failed-table` then migrate, and restart the worker.\n2. `queue.failed.driver` is set to `null` in config (common in a copied .env).\n3. The job throws in `__construct`, which happens on dispatch, not in the worker — that exception goes to your normal log, not the failed table.\n\nCheck `config(\'queue.failed\')` first; it is the cheapest of the three to rule out.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',106,120,0.2300,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:31:33'),(271,86,'user','It was the null driver. How do I get an alert when a job fails now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:33:00'),(272,86,'assistant','Hook the `JobFailed` event in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`:\n\n```php\nQueue::failing(function (JobFailed $event) {\n    Log::critical(\'Job failed\', [\n        \'connection\' => $event->connectionName,\n        \'job\' => $event->job->resolveName(),\n        \'exception\' => $event->exception->getMessage(),\n    ]);\n});\n```\n\nPoint that log channel at Slack or your error tracker rather than building a notification path of your own.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',317,105,0.4200,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:33:29'),(273,86,'user','Should I set tries or backoff on the job class?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:39:00'),(274,86,'assistant','Set both, and set them per job rather than globally. `public $tries = 3;` with `public array $backoff = [10, 60, 300];` gives you three attempts spread over roughly six minutes, which covers a provider blipping without hammering it. For anything that charges money on each attempt, use `$tries = 1` and requeue deliberately.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',532,81,0.6100,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-03 07:40:07'),(275,87,'user','I have a 600-line controller handling checkout. Where do I start splitting it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 17:15:00'),(276,87,'assistant','Split by reason to change, not by line count.\n\nStart with the three things a checkout controller usually conflates: request validation (move to a FormRequest), the payment call and its failure handling (a service), and the post-payment side effects — invoice, email, credits (listeners on one event). What is left in the controller should read as five or six lines you can take in at a glance.\n\nDo the FormRequest first: it is the lowest-risk move and it usually deletes 100 lines on its own.','claude-haiku-4-5',100,123,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 17:15:41'),(277,87,'user','How do I keep the refactor safe without a full test suite?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 17:19:00'),(278,87,'assistant','Write one characterisation test per branch you are about to move — it does not have to be pretty, only faithful to current behaviour. Then move code in commits small enough that reverting one is painless. Resist renaming anything in the same commit as a move; a diff that both moves and renames is unreviewable.','claude-haiku-4-5',315,78,0.7900,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 17:20:21'),(279,88,'user','What does /^(?!.*\\.\\.)[\\w.-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i actually match?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 12:30:00'),(280,88,'assistant','Piece by piece:\n\n- `(?!.*\\.\\.)` — a negative lookahead rejecting anything containing two consecutive dots.\n- `[\\w.-]+` — the local part: letters, digits, underscore, dot, hyphen.\n- `@[\\w-]+` — a domain label without dots.\n- `\\.[a-z]{2,}` — a dot and a TLD of two or more letters.\n- `/i` — case-insensitive.\n\nSo it matches `first.last@example.com` but not `a..b@example.com` or `user@sub.example.com` — note the single-label domain, which is probably a bug for you.','claude-sonnet-4-6',97,116,0.8500,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 12:30:48'),(281,89,'user','Rewrite this so it is shorter and less breathless: \"We are incredibly excited to finally announce the launch of our brand new AI-powered platform which we have been working on for many months.\"',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:26:00'),(282,89,'assistant','\"Our AI platform is live today.\"\n\nThat is the whole sentence. Excitement belongs in what the product does next, not in an adverb before it — readers discount \"incredibly excited\" automatically, and \"finally\" tells them you were late.\n\nIf you want one line of context: \"Our AI platform is live today, after eight months in private beta with 40 teams.\" The number does the work the adjectives were trying to do.','claude-haiku-4-5',129,103,0.4600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:27:25'),(283,89,'user','Now give me the opening paragraph that follows it.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:31:00'),(284,89,'assistant','\"It does one thing: turns the research you already have into publishable drafts, in your voice, in about a minute. No prompt engineering, no blank page. Paste your notes, pick a format, edit what comes back.\"\n\nThree sentences, one claim each — capability, cost, action. Everything else you want to say belongs further down, where the readers who are still with you will find it.','claude-haiku-4-5',313,95,0.8200,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 15:32:22'),(285,90,'user','Write a cold email to agency owners about our AI content tool. Every version I write sounds like spam.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 18:48:00'),(286,90,'assistant','It sounds like spam because it opens with you. Try:\n\n> Subject: your October case study\n>\n> Saw the retail piece you published last week — the before/after structure is the clearest I have read this year.\n>\n> We built something that produces that structure\'s first draft from a client interview transcript. Agencies using it cut drafting from a day to about forty minutes; the editing judgement stays yours.\n>\n> Worth fifteen minutes?\n\nOne specific observation, one concrete number, one small ask. The observation has to be real — that is the part that cannot be templated.','claude-sonnet-4-6',106,144,1.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 18:48:36'),(287,90,'user','What is a reasonable follow-up if they do not reply?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 18:53:00'),(288,90,'assistant','One follow-up, five business days later, shorter than the first and adding something rather than asking again:\n\n> Sent this over last week. Since then we published the transcript-to-draft walkthrough — here it is, in case it is more useful than a call.\n\nThen stop. A third email converts almost nobody and costs you the address.','claude-sonnet-4-6',313,82,1.5800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 18:53:20'),(289,91,'user','I have a 40-page quarterly report. What is the best way to get a usable summary out of it?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:00:00'),(290,91,'assistant','Do not ask for \"a summary\" — ask for the shape you need. Three that work:\n\n1. **Decision brief**: what changed, what it means, what someone must now decide. Half a page.\n2. **Number sheet**: every figure that moved more than 10%, with its prior value.\n3. **Exception list**: only what contradicts last quarter\'s expectations.\n\nGenerate all three from the same document and read the exception list first — that is where anything worth acting on hides.','gpt-5.6-terra',103,113,0.8600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:01:07'),(291,92,'user','A competitor launched at half our price and we are losing deals. How should we respond?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:14:00'),(292,92,'assistant','Do not match the price; you cannot win a race you started second in.\n\nFind the customers for whom the cheaper product is expensive — the ones whose cost of a bad output is high (regulated copy, client deliverables, anything with a legal review downstream). Reposition around that risk, not around features.\n\nConcretely: change the comparison page from a feature grid to a \"what happens when it is wrong\" section. Buyers who ignore that section were never going to pay your price anyway.','claude-haiku-4-5',102,122,0.4500,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:14:27'),(293,92,'user','Should sales be allowed to discount to close the gap?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:17:00'),(294,92,'assistant','Give them one lever, not a range: a fixed 15% for annual prepay, nothing else. An open discount ceiling teaches the market your list price is fiction, and the reps discover the floor within a quarter.','claude-haiku-4-5',314,50,0.7300,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:17:44'),(295,93,'user','Our landing page converts at 0.8%. The hero says \"The Future of Content, Powered by AI\". What is wrong?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 14:09:00'),(296,93,'assistant','The hero makes no claim a visitor can test. \"The future of content\" describes a category, not what happens if they sign up.\n\nReplace it with the outcome and the constraint: \"Publish a researched, on-brand article in under an hour — without hiring another writer.\" Then put the proof directly under it, not three sections down.\n\n0.8% on a page with a category-level headline is normal. Fixing the headline usually moves it more than any redesign will.','gemini-3.5-flash',106,113,0.6600,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 14:10:14'),(297,94,'user','Turn our post about AI writing workflows into a LinkedIn post that does not read like an ad.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:26:40'),(298,94,'assistant','> We measured where the hour actually goes when our team writes an article.\n>\n> Drafting: 12 minutes. Research: 20. Deciding what the piece is about: 28.\n>\n> We had automated the 12.\n>\n> Everything we have shipped since then targets the 28 — outline pressure-testing, angle comparison, source triage. It is less impressive in a demo and considerably more useful.\n\nOne surprising number, one admission, one implication. No CTA — comments do that work for you on LinkedIn.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',103,118,0.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:27:41'),(299,94,'user','Give me three hooks I could A/B against that opener.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:29:40'),(300,94,'assistant','1. \"Automating the writing was the easy part. It was also the wrong part.\"\n2. \"We timed every step of writing one article. The result changed our roadmap.\"\n3. \"Twelve minutes of drafting. Twenty-eight minutes of deciding. Guess which one we automated first.\"\n\nThe third tends to win because it makes the reader answer before they scroll.','llama-3.3-70b-versatile',313,85,0.4000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 13:30:01'),(301,95,'user','Plan two weeks of social posts leading into a launch. B2B SaaS, small audience.',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:54:00'),(302,95,'assistant','Week one, build the problem: three posts on the pain, each with a number or a story, none mentioning the product. One customer quote. One \"here is how we work today\" behind-the-scenes.\n\nWeek two, close the loop: teaser with a real screenshot (day 8), the reasoning behind one design decision (day 10), launch (day 12), then two days of answering replies in public rather than posting anything new.\n\nWith a small audience the replies matter more than the posts. Budget time for them.','claude-haiku-4-5',100,121,0.4400,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 11:54:31'),(303,96,'user','Monthly churn jumped from 3.1% to 5.4%. Where do I look first?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:18:00'),(304,96,'assistant','Segment before you theorise. Split the churned accounts by:\n\n- **Tenure** — a spike concentrated in month-two accounts is onboarding; spread evenly across tenures is pricing or a competitor.\n- **Plan** — if it is one tier, look at what changed in that tier\'s limits.\n- **Acquisition channel** — a bad cohort from one campaign can move the whole number.\n\nOne of those three almost always isolates it. If none do, check for an involuntary-churn spike: expired cards and failed renewals show up as churn but are a billing problem.','gemini-3.5-flash',96,132,0.6800,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:18:30'),(305,96,'user','It is concentrated in month two on the mid tier. What now?',NULL,0,0,0.0000,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:23:00'),(306,96,'assistant','That is an expectation gap, not a product gap: they bought something in month one and did not find it by month two.\n\nPull the last 20 churned mid-tier accounts and check which features they never touched. If a feature that justifies the tier\'s price went unused in 15 of 20, the onboarding never routed them to it — that is a sequencing fix, not a discount.','gemini-3.5-flash',315,90,1.2200,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-05 09:23:51');
